A profile keeps its extensions on a new Mac. Chrome checks protected preferences twice at load: first synchronously
with the legacy HMAC alone (an empty seed for this build, plus the machine's IOPlatformUUID), then, once the Safe
Storage key arrives, with the encrypted hash. The machine id changes when a profile moves to another Mac (Migration
Assistant, a Time Machine restore, a copied data directory), so every HMAC fails there and the first pass removed every
installed extension from extensions.settings, and reset developer mode, pinned tabs and startup pages, for good, before
the encrypted hash, which the migrated "Ardia Safe Storage" item still verifies, was read. In the field
(extensions_loaded, 14 days to 2026-11-26) every fresh drop checked was an install's first launch on different
hardware (M4 to M6, M4 Pro to M5 Pro, M1 to M5, M2 to M6, M3 Max to M5 Max), not an app update.

The first pass still resets whatever fails its HMAC before the preferences are published, so nothing unverified
reaches the extension loader or anything else at startup. What it resets that carries an encrypted hash is set aside
instead of lost: the value and its encrypted hash are kept in the protected store's own file under
ardia_awaiting_safe_storage (written by this filter on every write and never seen by the PrefService), and left off
prefs.tracked_preferences_reset. When the Safe Storage key arrives, the second pass checks each set-aside value against
its own encrypted hash with the same check Chrome applies to protected preferences. One that doesn't verify is dropped
and listed in prefs.tracked_preferences_reset, as before. One that does is given this machine's HMAC and put back by
the next launch's first pass, after that pass's own checks and before anything is published, only where nothing has
taken its place, with its encrypted hash and an HMAC stamped through the transaction, so it loads like any other
installed extension and the second pass checks it again. Nothing comes back in the session it was reset in: an
extension entry the loader never registered breaks shutdown (PreferenceAPI clears content settings for every
extension in the preferences). Until the key arrives nothing is judged and nothing waits for it: with the Keychain
locked or access denied, or an encryptor with no usable key, the values stay set aside for a later launch and
startup goes on without them. With kEncryptedPrefHashing off, or without OSCrypt, there is no second pass and nothing
is set aside. The reset list is now posted to the PrefService before the second pass is, so a reset the second pass
lists isn't overwritten by the first pass's list when the key is already there.

